A||||18||Dan86gt3.8||anyone have slow electric windows?||||1||
Z||000000||Dan86gt3.8||01-13-2000||04:05 AM||||yes ,y power windows roll up very slow when the engine is off and don't exactly move quick while the cra is on.  Is this a common problem?  I herd it is something to do with the pop-rivits they are held in with.  Does anyone know a fix for this?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||01-13-2000||04:16 AM||||I don't know what it would have to do with the rivets.  I gutted a couple of doors and don't see anything wrong with the rivets.  They don't seem to be in the way or anything.  Try checking your contacts at the plug to motor connection.  Maybe you just need new motors.  I just happen to have a pair for sale.  One looks almost new and the other is obviously original.  Also try greasing everything.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||01-13-2000||09:15 AM||||If the rivits were worn, it could cause one of the many moving parts to bind a bit, which would slow the motors, but the most likely cause is the motors are showing their age.||||reg||1
Z||000003||turbo86se||01-13-2000||11:14 AM||||I know this may be far off, but check your battery. A good friend of mine is a Fiero-only mechanic. When he services Fieros that have been sitting in his shop for a while without starting, the windows are so slow it's agonizing!!  But Fieros that get driven everyday like his Ferrari Dino kit car (86GT now for sale), and his 88 Formula, the power window are quick.<P>I have YET to own a Fiero with power windows!!!<P>I do remember a smashed and trashed gold 86SE a while back, that sat and sat in a towing company's side lot. One day I stopped to check it out. The shop guys gave me the keys. The battery was nearly dead, lights very dim, and the power windows were soooooo slow!!!  Like I said, I'm probably far off, but just my .02!  take care,  joel||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-13-2000||02:05 PM||||I had an old set of doors with power windows sitting outside for a few years, then I got my Formula with manual windows soooo I decided to swap the doors. Those windows were very slow when I checked them for funtion- greased the tracks and the gear quadrant and it made quite a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||01-13-2000||05:43 PM||||M o s t  e v e r y o n e  o n  t h i s  F o r u m  i s     S  L  O  W    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>That includes ME   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>OK most window motors are strong just dirty, Clean, Clean, and Clean again!<BR>Then LUBE, LUBE, LUBE (Monkeyman don't say it)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||lawrence||01-13-2000||08:24 PM||||Hello Folks,<P>I just joined the other day, so this is my first posting.<P>I would agree with what dogtired said.  Dirty tracks can really slow down the windows.<P>I would also add that the wiring in the Fiero isn't adequate for the window motors.  I recently added a one-touch control circuit to my car, so while I was at it, I ran a #8 wire from the battery to the window relays.  I then used  #12 wire on each relay so that there was ample current available to drive the motors.<P>After that, my windows took about a second less to go full stroke.  Running this wire wasn't the easiest thing to do, but it would probably be much harder to rewind the window motors for more torque.<P>-L||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||01-13-2000||08:35 PM||||Windows would most likely go slower when the enigne isn't running. With the engine off, all the motors get is battery juice, but with the engine on, they also have the alternator giving power. I would go with cleaning the motors.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rcn491||01-13-2000||09:56 PM||||Are they only slow with the door closed ? If so the glass can be adjusted so there is less pressure on the rubber seal. That was the problem I found with mine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||01-14-2000||06:33 AM||||Lawrence,<P>Welcome to the Forum.<P>Did the relays come with the One-touch system you installed?  I'm converting my manual window/door lock 85SE to power and don't recall any relays in the stock power window circuit.  I got a pair of pw/pdl doors at Ed Park's swap meet last year for $40.00.  I got the switchesand plate and the correct door storage pockets from Ed and I pulled the wiring connectors, pdl relay and what I could get of the harness out of a wreck.<P>JC Whitney used to carry a one touch system but they don't anymore.  Where did you get yours?  Is it part of an alarm system?<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||lawrence||01-14-2000||12:43 PM||||Carroll,<P>Yes, the relays are part of the One-Touch circuit that I designed and installed this Summer.  They operate the up and down on both windows.  This is really handy especially with the slow windows the Fiero has.<P>I know that you already have the wiring harness, but you might consider making a new one with heavier wires.  I thought about doing this, but I didn't want to dig into the car that deep.<P>Good luck,<P>-L||||reg||1
Z||000011||dogtired||01-14-2000||01:03 PM||||lawrence:<BR>WELCOME  WELCOME WELCOME<BR>Always glad to see New folks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Could you post a little more, ( well a whole bunch ) more information about the system you designed???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What is involved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> What it CO$TS  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> Could the average dope like me do it??? <BR>Again Welcome!<BR>See how we feed of each other??   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Just curious!||||reg||6
Z||000012||RZURCTN||01-14-2000||05:36 PM||||A side note to the cleaning thing.  While parting out an 84 Fiero, I noticed that a lot of the grease was getting really gummy!  Given the age of our cars that is to be expected.  Grease gets hard as it gets old.  Clean off the old grease and put in some fresh stuff.  Just my $0.02!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000013||lawrence||01-14-2000||09:03 PM||||You can read about the On-Touch windows in the e-mag I publish called WebElectric Magazine.  The article is in this issue:<P>  <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm</A> <P>As far as cost, a bit high because of the relays.  You need relays that are rated for Direct Current, so they are a little pricey.  Other than that, it's mostly just a lot of work.<P>If you have any more questions, just ask.<P>-L||||reg||1
